WEBVTT

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:04.265
Hypothesis-driven hunting turns a broad concern into something an analyst can test.

00:00:04.265 --> 00:00:07.840
A vague suspicion sounds like, something may be wrong with privileged accounts.

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:09.920
A testable hypothesis is more specific:

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:12.870
if an adversary is using compromised privileged credentials,

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:16.650
then authentication logs may show privileged accounts accessing unusual systems,

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:20.385
from unusual source locations, outside normal administrative windows,

00:00:20.385 --> 00:00:21.850
during the last fourteen days.

00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:26.260
That statement can guide data collection and analysis. A practical hunt hypothesis usually

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:27.800
includes five parts.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:31.165
First, suspected behavior: what activity might be happening?

00:00:31.165 --> 00:00:35.505
Second, affected assets or identities: which systems, users, groups, cloud accounts,

00:00:35.505 --> 00:00:37.100
or network segments are in scope?

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:41.200
Third, required telemetry: which logs or sensors should show the behavior if it occurred?

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:45.270
Fourth, expected observations: what patterns would support the hypothesis?

00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:49.345
Fifth, decision criteria: what evidence would make the analyst escalate, reject,

00:00:49.345 --> 00:00:50.335
or refine the hunt?

00:00:50.335 --> 00:00:53.280
Decision criteria are important because hunting can drift.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:54.225
Without criteria,

00:00:54.225 --> 00:00:58.310
an analyst may keep pivoting forever or treat any unusual event as meaningful.

00:00:58.310 --> 00:01:01.360
Criteria do not have to be rigid, but they should be explicit.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:02.080
For example,

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:06.280
the hunt may escalate if a privileged account authenticates to a sensitive server from a

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:10.045
source IP not used by that account in the prior thirty days and the activity has no

00:01:10.045 --> 00:01:11.230
approved change ticket.

00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:15.500
The hunt may reject the hypothesis if all unusual logons map to known maintenance windows

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:16.649
and service accounts.

00:01:16.649 --> 00:01:18.710
Hypotheses can be confirmed, rejected,

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:21.885
or refined. Confirmed does not always mean confirmed malicious.

00:01:21.885 --> 00:01:25.810
It may mean the expected behavior was observed and needs validation. Rejected does not

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:27.600
mean the environment is safe forever.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:31.460
It means the available evidence did not support that hypothesis in that scope and time

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:31.977
window.

00:01:31.977 --> 00:01:35.120
Refined means the initial idea was too broad, too narrow,

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:36.875
or pointed at the wrong telemetry,

00:01:36.875 --> 00:01:39.245
and the analyst learned enough to ask a better question.

00:01:39.245 --> 00:01:41.790
Good hypotheses are also honest about telemetry.

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:44.555
If endpoint process data is missing from half the fleet,

00:01:44.555 --> 00:01:49.286
a hypothesis about process execution cannot be fully tested across all endpoints.

00:01:49.286 --> 00:01:51.360
If DNS logs only retain seven days,

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:54.480
a thirty-day hunt needs a different source or a narrower conclusion.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:57.510
These limitations should appear in the hunt notes. For beginners,

00:01:57.510 --> 00:01:59.332
the value of a hypothesis is focus.

00:01:59.332 --> 00:02:03.270
It tells you which data to query, which pivots are reasonable, which assumptions matter,

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:05.320
and what outcome would change your confidence.

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:07.600
It also makes the hunt reviewable by another analyst.

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:12.040
A documented hypothesis turns hunting from personal intuition into a repeatable defensive

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:12.566
workflow.

00:02:12.566 --> 00:02:15.725
Hypotheses should be small enough to test with the available data.

00:02:15.725 --> 00:02:18.850
If the suspected behavior covers every platform, every identity,

00:02:18.850 --> 00:02:21.505
and every possible adversary objective, break it down.

00:02:21.505 --> 00:02:25.935
A focused hypothesis about one behavior in one environment is usually more productive than

00:02:25.935 --> 00:02:28.220
a sweeping statement that cannot be validated.

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:31.040
Scope gives the analyst a way to finish the work responsibly.
